An Unattend.xml (or answer file) is the key to fully automating the deployment process. Placing this file in the correct location allows Windows to skip privacy prompts, region selection, and account creation during the Out-of-Box Experience (OOBE).
Here is a fully functional, production-ready Unattend.xml template, along with instructions on how to use it.

Step 1: Create the Unattend.xml File

Copy the XML block below, paste it into a text editor (like Notepad), and save it exactly as unattend.xml (make sure it doesn't end in .txt).
xml
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<unattend xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:unattend">
    <!-- 1. Specialize Pass: Configures computer-specific settings -->
    <settings pass="specialize">
        <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Shell-Setup" processorArchitecture="amd64" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS" xmlns:wcm="http://microsoft.com" xmlns:xsi="http://w3.org">
            <ComputerName>*</ComputerName> <!-- Auto-generates a random computer name -->
            <TimeZone>Eastern Standard Time</TimeZone> <!-- Change to your preferred Time Zone -->
        </component>
    </settings>

    <!-- 2. OOBE System Pass: Automates the setup screens -->
    <settings pass="oobeSystem">
        <component name="Microsoft-Windows-International-Core" processorArchitecture="amd64" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS" xmlns:wcm="http://microsoft.com" xmlns:xsi="http://w3.org">
            <InputLocale>en-US</InputLocale>
            <SystemLocale>en-US</SystemLocale>
            <UILanguage>en-US</UILanguage>
            <UserLocale>en-US</UserLocale>
        </component>
        <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Shell-Setup" processorArchitecture="amd64" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS" xmlns:wcm="http://microsoft.com" xmlns:xsi="http://w3.org">
            <OOBE>
                <HideEULAPage>true</HideEULAPage>
                <HideLocalAccountScreen>false</HideLocalAccountScreen> <!-- Stops here to let you name the final user account -->
                <HideOnlineAccountScreen>true</HideOnlineAccountScreen>
                <HideWirelessSetupInOOBE>true</HideWirelessSetupInOOBE>
                <NetworkLocation>Work</NetworkLocation>
                <ProtectYourPC>1</ProtectYourPC> <!-- Express/Recommended security settings -->
                <SkipMachineOOBE>false</SkipMachineOOBE>
                <SkipUserOOBE>false</SkipUserOOBE>
            </OOBE>
            
            <!-- Optional: Uncomment this section if you want to force-create a local administrator account automatically -->
            <!--
            <UserAccounts>
                <LocalAccounts>
                    <LocalAccount wcm:action="add">
                        <Password>
                            <Value>Password123!</Value>
                            <PlainText>true</PlainText>
                        </Password>
                        <Description>Local Admin Account</Description>
                        <DisplayName>Administrator</DisplayName>
                        <Group>Administrators</Group>
                        <Name>Admin</Name>
                    </LocalAccount>
                </LocalAccounts>
            </UserAccounts>
            -->
        </component>
    </settings>
</unattend>
Use code with caution.

Step 2: How to Implement the File

You have two options for where to place this file, depending on how you want to bake it into your manual imaging pipeline:

Option A: Inject it during Sysprep (Recommended for WIM Capture)

If you want the answer file hardcoded directly inside your custom image so it triggers no matter how the WIM is deployed:
1. Copy the unattend.xml file into your Virtual Machine's C:\Windows\System32\Sysprep\ directory while still in Audit Mode.
2. Run Sysprep via the command line, pointing directly to the file:cmd
net stop tiledatamodelsvc /y
C:\Windows\System32\Sysprep\sysprep.exe /generalize /oobe /shutdown /unattend:C:\Windows\System32\Sysprep\unattend.xml
Use code with caution.
3. Once the VM shuts down, boot to WinPE and capture your image as detailed in the previous step.

Option B: Root of the Installation Media (Easiest for ISOs)

If you already captured your install.wim and don't want to re-capture it, you can place the answer file directly onto your installation media:
1. Open your extracted ISO file directory on your technician machine (e.g., C:\OS_Files\).
2. Paste the unattend.xml file directly into the root folder (C:\OS_Files\unattend.xml).
3. Re-compile the ISO using the oscdimg tool provided in Part 2. The Windows installer will automatically search the root of the drive for this file and apply it during setup.
