To automate actions immediately after the first user logs in, you can use the FirstLogonCommands section. This is ideal for activating Windows, running cleanup scripts, or tweaking registry keys without manual intervention.

Step 1: Updated Unattend.xml (With FirstLogonCommands)

This updated template includes an automated section that sequentially runs commands right as the desktop loads for the first time.
Replace your existing file content with this layout:
xml
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<unattend xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:unattend">
    <!-- 1. Specialize Pass: Configures computer-specific settings -->
    <settings pass="specialize">
        <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Shell-Setup" processorArchitecture="amd64" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS" xmlns:wcm="http://microsoft.com" xmlns:xsi="http://w3.org">
            <ComputerName>*</ComputerName>
            <TimeZone>Eastern Standard Time</TimeZone> <!-- Change to your local Time Zone -->
        </component>
    </settings>

    <!-- 2. OOBE System Pass: Automates setup screens and first logon -->
    <settings pass="oobeSystem">
        <component name="Microsoft-Windows-International-Core" processorArchitecture="amd64" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS" xmlns:wcm="http://microsoft.com" xmlns:xsi="http://w3.org">
            <InputLocale>en-US</InputLocale>
            <SystemLocale>en-US</SystemLocale>
            <UILanguage>en-US</UILanguage>
            <UserLocale>en-US</UserLocale>
        </component>
        <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Shell-Setup" processorArchitecture="amd64" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS" xmlns:wcm="http://microsoft.com" xmlns:xsi="http://w3.org">
            <OOBE>
                <HideEULAPage>true</HideEULAPage>
                <HideLocalAccountScreen>false</HideLocalAccountScreen>
                <HideOnlineAccountScreen>true</HideOnlineAccountScreen>
                <HideWirelessSetupInOOBE>true</HideWirelessSetupInOOBE>
                <NetworkLocation>Work</NetworkLocation>
                <ProtectYourPC>1</ProtectYourPC>
                <SkipMachineOOBE>false</SkipMachineOOBE>
                <SkipUserOOBE>false</SkipUserOOBE>
            </OOBE>
            
            <!-- First Logon Commands: Executes sequentially upon first desktop load -->
            <FirstLogonCommands>
                <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
                    <Order>1</Order>
                    <Description>Disable Windows Consumer Features (Prevents Candy Crush/TikTok shortcuts)</Description>
                    <CommandLine>reg add "HKLM\Software\Policies\Microsoft\Windows\CloudContent" /v DisableWindowsConsumerFeatures /t REG_DWORD /d 1 /f</CommandLine>
                </SynchronousCommand>
                <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
                    <Order>2</Order>
                    <Description>Attempt Windows Activation Silently</Description>
                    <CommandLine>cscript //B C:\Windows\System32\slmgr.vbs /ato</CommandLine>
                </SynchronousCommand>
                <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
                    <Order>3</Order>
                    <Description>Run custom post-setup cleanup script (Optional)</Description>
                    <CommandLine>powershell.exe -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File C:\Windows\Setup\Scripts\PostLogon.ps1</CommandLine>
                </SynchronousCommand>
            </FirstLogonCommands>
        </component>
    </settings>
</unattend>
Use code with caution.

Step 2: Advanced Customization (Using the PostLogon.ps1 Script)

Notice how Order 3 calls a script located at C:\Windows\Setup\Scripts\PostLogon.ps1.
Instead of cluttering your XML with long string commands, creating a master PowerShell script file inside your image before you run Sysprep is highly recommended. While in Audit Mode, create that directory path and drop a cleanup script like this into it:
powershell
# C:\Windows\Setup\Scripts\PostLogon.ps1

# 1. Force check and trigger pending developer/system updates
wuauclt /detectnow /updatenow

# 2. Delete temporary installation logs/Chocolatey setup caches
if (Test-Path "C:\ProgramData\chocolatey\lib-bak") { Remove-Item "C:\ProgramData\chocolatey\lib-bak" -Recycle -Force }
Remove-Item "C:\Windows\TEMP\*" -Recycle -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue

# 3. Optimize the hard drive or SSD layout post-deployment
Optimize-Volume -DriveLetter C -Defrag -Verbose
Use code with caution.

Important Execution Notes

• Privilege Level: FirstLogonCommands run elevated in the system/administrator context, so they bypass standard user execution blocks.
• Timing: They run before the user interacts with the desktop. If a script requires a user profile directory to exist (like modifying a specific desktop wallpaper path), make sure you add a slight start sleep delay (Start-Sleep -Seconds 5) at the top of your custom .ps1 file.
